1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,190
In the last week of January
nineteen ninety-five,

2
00:00:04,209 --> 00:00:09,330
the auditors of a small futures office in
Singapore are looking at a hole in its

3
00:00:09,349 --> 00:00:10,150
books.

4
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:16,690
It is worth seven point seven billion yen,
more than a hundred million Singapore

5
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:20,359
dollars, and nobody can
say where the money went.

6
00:00:21,196 --> 00:00:23,905
The office's general
manager has an explanation.

7
00:00:24,736 --> 00:00:28,606
Then he has a second one,
and a third, and a fourth.

8
00:00:29,636 --> 00:00:34,975
In the version the auditors finally accept,
the money is simply owed by an American

9
00:00:34,996 --> 00:00:39,286
brokerage firm, and it will be paid
back on the second of February.

10
00:00:40,234 --> 00:00:44,225
To prove it, he produces a
fax from the American firm.

11
00:00:45,314 --> 00:00:50,004
In the upper right corner of that fax,
where a sender's name usually sits,

12
00:00:50,375 --> 00:00:52,884
it says Nick and Lisa.

13
00:00:53,935 --> 00:00:57,284
He had sent it from the fax
machine in his own apartment.

14
00:00:58,296 --> 00:01:04,856
Nick is Nick Leeson, twenty-seven
years old, the star trader of Barings,

15
00:01:04,876 --> 00:01:06,847
Britain's oldest merchant bank.

16
00:01:08,156 --> 00:01:09,486
Lisa is his wife.

17
00:01:10,436 --> 00:01:12,507
The auditors sign off anyway.

18
00:01:13,522 --> 00:01:17,462
Barings had been founded
in seventeen sixty-two.

19
00:01:17,482 --> 00:01:22,233
It had financed the Louisiana Purchase,
and according to the Associated Press,

20
00:01:22,703 --> 00:01:25,992
it counted Queen Elizabeth
the Second among its clients.

21
00:01:27,039 --> 00:01:30,569
Four weeks after that
fax, the bank was gone.

22
00:01:31,439 --> 00:01:35,149
The losses came to eight hundred
twenty-seven million pounds,

23
00:01:35,459 --> 00:01:40,219
about one point three billion dollars,
twice the capital the bank had available

24
00:01:40,239 --> 00:01:41,129
for trading.

25
00:01:42,069 --> 00:01:47,120
A Dutch group bought what was
left of it for one pound.

26
00:01:47,519 --> 00:01:52,899
All of those losses had sat in one account,
an account with a number anyone could

27
00:01:52,919 --> 00:01:53,719
remember.

28
00:01:54,579 --> 00:01:59,759
And for two and a half years, the people
sending him the money did not ask what

29
00:01:59,779 --> 00:02:00,589
was in it.

30
00:02:02,227 --> 00:02:05,457
Nick Leeson grew up on a
council estate in Watford,

31
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:06,567
north of London.

32
00:02:07,287 --> 00:02:10,296
His father was a plasterer,
his mother a nurse.

33
00:02:11,127 --> 00:02:15,966
He left school in nineteen eighty-five
and got a job as a clerk at Coutts,

34
00:02:15,986 --> 00:02:17,981
settling paper cheques.

35
00:02:18,382 --> 00:02:23,852
Two years later he moved to the futures
and options back office at Morgan Stanley,

36
00:02:24,382 --> 00:02:28,252
where the job was clearing and
settling trades other people made.

37
00:02:29,421 --> 00:02:34,822
In nineteen eighty-nine he joined Barings,
on a salary of twelve thousand pounds

38
00:02:34,842 --> 00:02:35,812
a year.

39
00:02:36,786 --> 00:02:40,676
Barings sent him to Jakarta to
sort out its back office there,

40
00:02:41,105 --> 00:02:46,085
a pile of unpaid share certificates
worth about a hundred million pounds.

41
00:02:46,105 --> 00:02:49,855
In Jakarta he met a
colleague named Lisa Sims.

42
00:02:50,786 --> 00:02:53,861
They married in March nineteen ninety-two.

43
00:02:54,260 --> 00:02:58,780
That same year, regulators in Britain
refused him a broker's licence,

44
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:03,730
because his application had left out
a court judgment for an unpaid debt.

45
00:03:04,721 --> 00:03:09,971
When he applied for a licence in Singapore,
neither he nor Barings mentioned it.

46
00:03:11,051 --> 00:03:16,311
In April nineteen ninety-two, Barings
decided to start trading on the Singapore

47
00:03:16,331 --> 00:03:20,141
International Monetary
Exchange, known as SIMEX.

48
00:03:20,852 --> 00:03:23,382
It sent Leeson out as general manager.

49
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:27,230
The business was meant
to be almost boring.

50
00:03:27,881 --> 00:03:33,270
The same Japanese stock market contract,
the Nikkei two-two-five future,

51
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:39,371
traded in Osaka and in Singapore, and
the prices sometimes differed by a hair.

52
00:03:40,161 --> 00:03:44,170
Buy in one place, sell in the
other, keep the difference.

53
00:03:45,221 --> 00:03:47,191
Almost no risk.

54
00:03:48,495 --> 00:03:51,885
But Leeson was put in charge
of both sides of the office.

55
00:03:52,615 --> 00:03:57,275
He ran the traders on the floor, and he ran
the back office that recorded what they

56
00:03:57,295 --> 00:03:58,095
did.

57
00:03:58,495 --> 00:04:02,865
The man placing the bets was
also the man keeping score.

58
00:04:03,867 --> 00:04:08,827
Years later, in his memoir, he described
the feeling of finally getting out of the

59
00:04:08,847 --> 00:04:10,858
back office and onto the floor.

60
00:04:11,768 --> 00:04:16,337
The money, he wrote, was hanging
in the air right in front of him,

61
00:04:16,707 --> 00:04:21,617
invisible but highly charged,
just waiting to be earthed.

62
00:04:23,367 --> 00:04:28,667
Every trading office has an error account,
a place to park the small mistakes that

63
00:04:28,687 --> 00:04:32,137
happen when people shout
orders across a crowded floor.

64
00:04:33,027 --> 00:04:37,036
In July nineteen ninety-two,
Leeson's office got one.

65
00:04:37,826 --> 00:04:41,957
Its number was eight
eight eight eight eight.

66
00:04:42,357 --> 00:04:46,887
By his own account, the first thing he
hid there was a junior trader's slip.

67
00:04:47,437 --> 00:04:51,206
An order for twenty contracts
went through the wrong way round,

68
00:04:51,557 --> 00:04:53,867
and it cost twenty thousand pounds.

69
00:04:55,137 --> 00:04:57,966
Instead of reporting it, he parked it.

70
00:04:58,903 --> 00:05:00,473
Then came a bigger one.

71
00:05:01,043 --> 00:05:06,353
In his telling, he forgot to reconcile a
discrepancy of five hundred contracts,

72
00:05:06,603 --> 00:05:10,463
and it cost one point
seven million dollars.

73
00:05:10,483 --> 00:05:14,853
That, he later said, was when
he crossed into real crime,

74
00:05:15,463 --> 00:05:19,293
because the only way to keep
his job was to hide it too.

75
00:05:20,288 --> 00:05:23,398
Then he started trading
with the bank's own money,

76
00:05:23,889 --> 00:05:26,398
betting on where the
Japanese market would go.

77
00:05:27,368 --> 00:05:33,869
When a bet lost, he hid the loss in eight
eight eight eight eight and bet bigger

78
00:05:33,889 --> 00:05:34,829
to win it back.

79
00:05:34,848 --> 00:05:38,878
Every loss made the next bet bigger.

80
00:05:39,963 --> 00:05:44,944
By the end of nineteen ninety-two, the
account held more than two million pounds

81
00:05:44,963 --> 00:05:45,893
in losses.

82
00:05:46,944 --> 00:05:50,733
By the end of nineteen ninety-three,
about twenty-three million.

83
00:05:51,743 --> 00:05:56,453
By the end of nineteen ninety-four,
more than two hundred million.

84
00:05:57,684 --> 00:06:00,754
And yet, on paper, he was a sensation.

85
00:06:01,704 --> 00:06:07,054
In nineteen ninety-three, his desk was
credited with more than ten million pounds,

86
00:06:07,604 --> 00:06:10,074
about a tenth of the whole bank's profit.

87
00:06:10,864 --> 00:06:13,874
His salary was fifty thousand pounds.

88
00:06:14,344 --> 00:06:17,314
His bonus was a hundred
and thirty thousand.

89
00:06:18,238 --> 00:06:22,588
According to the inquiry, as summarised
by Australia's central bank,

90
00:06:23,158 --> 00:06:27,718
more than sixty percent of the revenue of
Barings' worldwide derivatives business

91
00:06:27,738 --> 00:06:32,888
was being generated out of Leeson's
supposedly riskless trading.

92
00:06:33,998 --> 00:06:39,708
Nobody in London asked how a strategy
with almost no risk could earn that much.

93
00:06:41,041 --> 00:06:43,791
The trick, according to
the Singapore inspectors,

94
00:06:44,121 --> 00:06:45,031
was timing.

95
00:06:46,001 --> 00:06:50,721
At the end of each month, his settlement
staff booked entries that made the account

96
00:06:50,741 --> 00:06:53,771
look empty, and reversed them afterwards.

97
00:06:54,741 --> 00:07:00,101
He also sold options at month-end, collecting
premiums to cover the hole for the

98
00:07:00,121 --> 00:07:01,731
day the report was printed.

99
00:07:02,574 --> 00:07:03,604
There were warnings.

100
00:07:04,474 --> 00:07:09,694
In September nineteen ninety-three, the
exchange itself wrote to Barings listing

101
00:07:09,714 --> 00:07:14,254
fifteen breaches of its rules, and attached
a trade ticket from account eight eight

102
00:07:14,274 --> 00:07:16,765
eight eight eight.

103
00:07:17,165 --> 00:07:22,725
In nineteen ninety-four, the bank's own
internal auditors recommended that he should

104
00:07:22,745 --> 00:07:24,715
no longer run the back office.

105
00:07:25,846 --> 00:07:27,875
Barings did not implement it.

106
00:07:28,529 --> 00:07:30,819
The biggest warning was the money.

107
00:07:31,809 --> 00:07:35,039
Leeson's losses had to be covered
with cash at the exchange,

108
00:07:35,309 --> 00:07:37,399
and he got that cash from London.

109
00:07:38,369 --> 00:07:41,039
London believed it was
lending to his clients.

110
00:07:41,689 --> 00:07:47,149
By the account of the inquiry, Barings was
apparently lending more than three hundred

111
00:07:47,169 --> 00:07:52,799
million pounds to clients, against
collateral of about thirty-one million.

112
00:07:53,935 --> 00:07:57,596
Three days after Christmas
nineteen ninety-four,

113
00:07:57,615 --> 00:08:02,576
a clerk in London's settlements department
wrote that the best case was a spreadsheet

114
00:08:02,596 --> 00:08:03,396
error.

115
00:08:03,895 --> 00:08:08,065
The worst case, he wrote,
was that it's plain rubbish.

116
00:08:09,415 --> 00:08:11,165
The money kept going.

117
00:08:13,043 --> 00:08:18,563
By January nineteen ninety-five, one of
his biggest positions was a bet that the

118
00:08:18,583 --> 00:08:20,853
Japanese market would stay calm.

119
00:08:21,663 --> 00:08:24,112
It is called a short straddle.

120
00:08:24,763 --> 00:08:35,363
He collects money up front, and he keeps
it as long as the Nikkei barely moves.

121
00:08:35,383 --> 00:08:36,182
If it moves a lot, in either
direction, the losses have no ceiling.

122
00:08:37,067 --> 00:08:41,137
At five forty-six in the morning
on the seventeenth of January,

123
00:08:41,827 --> 00:08:47,277
an earthquake of magnitude six point
nine struck the area around Kobe.

124
00:08:48,467 --> 00:08:51,117
At least five thousand people died.

125
00:08:52,294 --> 00:08:53,804
Highways collapsed.

126
00:08:54,514 --> 00:08:56,004
Whole districts burned.

127
00:08:57,014 --> 00:09:00,375
In the days that followed, the
Japanese stock market fell,

128
00:09:00,394 --> 00:09:02,264
at times sharply.

129
00:09:03,219 --> 00:09:08,548
A trader with a calm-market bet now
had the opposite of a calm market.

130
00:09:09,499 --> 00:09:11,489
Leeson did not close the position.

131
00:09:12,139 --> 00:09:16,749
He bought Nikkei futures instead,
enormous amounts of them,

132
00:09:16,979 --> 00:09:21,869
betting that the market would bounce back
quickly and take his losses with it.

133
00:09:22,503 --> 00:09:24,793
For a moment, it nearly worked.

134
00:09:25,802 --> 00:09:29,432
According to the Reserve Bank of
Australia's summary of the British inquiry,

135
00:09:29,903 --> 00:09:34,503
at one point in early February he had
recovered the losses he had made since the

136
00:09:34,523 --> 00:09:35,593
start of the year.

137
00:09:36,062 --> 00:09:42,723
Then, in the last two weeks of February,
every market he was in turned against him

138
00:09:42,743 --> 00:09:43,673
at once.

139
00:09:44,682 --> 00:09:48,192
The requests to London
grew with every move.

140
00:09:49,022 --> 00:09:53,362
At the end of nineteen ninety-four, his
office had received about three hundred

141
00:09:53,382 --> 00:09:55,211
fifty million dollars.

142
00:09:56,042 --> 00:10:01,631
By the twenty-fourth of February, the figure
stood at one point one eight billion.

143
00:10:02,302 --> 00:10:06,772
About a billion Singapore dollars went
out in the last three weeks alone.

144
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:10,650
These were the same weeks
as the hole in the books,

145
00:10:11,081 --> 00:10:14,810
the four stories and the fax
that said Nick and Lisa.

146
00:10:15,961 --> 00:10:20,230
On the third of February, the auditors
forwarded their clearance to London.

147
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:25,880
When some managers there grew worried, they
were told the matter was being handled

148
00:10:25,900 --> 00:10:27,410
at the highest levels.

149
00:10:28,590 --> 00:10:32,940
London had even set up an internal
review of the Singapore funding.

150
00:10:33,730 --> 00:10:36,259
It was called the Singapore Project.

151
00:10:37,550 --> 00:10:41,000
Leeson took it over, and
ended it with a vague memo.

152
00:10:41,957 --> 00:10:48,077
By the end, the money sent to one office
in Singapore was twice the capital of the

153
00:10:48,097 --> 00:10:49,687
entire Barings group.

154
00:10:50,817 --> 00:10:52,767
There was nothing left to send.

155
00:10:54,711 --> 00:11:00,291
On Thursday the twenty-third of February,
Nick and Lisa Leeson left their luxury

156
00:11:00,311 --> 00:11:01,721
apartment in Singapore.

157
00:11:02,551 --> 00:11:05,081
The laundry was still
drying on the balcony.

158
00:11:05,951 --> 00:11:07,280
He left a note behind.

159
00:11:07,911 --> 00:11:10,061
It said I'm sorry.

160
00:11:11,013 --> 00:11:16,713
They drove across the border into Malaysia,
then went on to a resort in Kota Kinabalu,

161
00:11:16,733 --> 00:11:21,562
on the island of Borneo, almost
fifteen hundred kilometres away.

162
00:11:22,572 --> 00:11:26,719
On the twenty-fifth of February,
he turned twenty-eight.

163
00:11:27,119 --> 00:11:30,989
That weekend, the Bank of England
tried to arrange a rescue.

164
00:11:31,739 --> 00:11:32,538
It failed.

165
00:11:33,538 --> 00:11:38,119
The Chancellor, Kenneth Clarke, said it
would have been wrong to use public money

166
00:11:38,139 --> 00:11:40,087
to save Barings.

167
00:11:40,488 --> 00:11:45,677
On Sunday the twenty-sixth of February,
Barings was declared insolvent.

168
00:11:46,408 --> 00:11:51,588
A bank that had survived wars, revolutions
and its own near-collapse in eighteen

169
00:11:51,608 --> 00:11:56,498
ninety was finished by one
office and one account.

170
00:11:57,375 --> 00:12:00,375
On Tuesday, according to the
Malaysian Daily Express,

171
00:12:00,395 --> 00:12:05,475
Leeson walked into the Royal Brunei
Airlines office in Kota Kinabalu,

172
00:12:05,495 --> 00:12:08,025
asked for the next
available flight to Europe,

173
00:12:08,275 --> 00:12:10,925
and paid fifteen hundred dollars in cash.

174
00:12:11,772 --> 00:12:17,272
The plane left on Wednesday night, before
Malaysian immigration could act on a police

175
00:12:17,292 --> 00:12:18,742
request to hold him.

176
00:12:19,592 --> 00:12:24,772
It stopped in Bangkok, where a newspaper
editor phoned the airport and tried to

177
00:12:24,792 --> 00:12:25,591
have it held.

178
00:12:26,472 --> 00:12:32,852
The duty manager told him, please don't
ask me to go to the runway and try to stop

179
00:12:32,872 --> 00:12:33,672
the plane.

180
00:12:34,683 --> 00:12:39,274
Early on Thursday the second of
March, the plane landed in Frankfurt.

181
00:12:40,243 --> 00:12:43,624
German police carrying photographs
of the couple came on board.

182
00:12:43,644 --> 00:12:46,493
Leeson was taken into custody.

183
00:12:47,403 --> 00:12:50,133
Lisa was released and
flew home to Britain.

184
00:12:51,181 --> 00:12:56,151
He told the police, again and again,
that he wanted to go back to Britain.

185
00:12:57,240 --> 00:12:59,230
Singapore wanted him back first.

186
00:13:00,346 --> 00:13:05,015
His newly hired German lawyer said
he had spoken to Leeson's parents.

187
00:13:05,745 --> 00:13:08,535
Asked whether they were happy
their son had been found,

188
00:13:08,966 --> 00:13:12,936
the lawyer paused, and
answered, I don't think so.

189
00:13:14,857 --> 00:13:18,587
He spent nine months in a German
jail fighting extradition.

190
00:13:19,637 --> 00:13:24,637
He gave up after a Singapore investigation
found fault with senior Barings managers

191
00:13:24,657 --> 00:13:25,467
as well.

192
00:13:26,370 --> 00:13:29,769
On the twenty-third of November,
he flew back to Singapore,

193
00:13:29,789 --> 00:13:33,100
twelve and a half hours
on Singapore Airlines.

194
00:13:34,169 --> 00:13:38,980
According to the Associated Press,
the in-flight film was Bad Boys.

195
00:13:39,629 --> 00:13:43,550
He walked out at Changi airport in
a baseball cap turned backwards,

196
00:13:43,570 --> 00:13:47,819
and was arrested on eleven
counts of forgery and cheating.

197
00:13:48,841 --> 00:13:53,221
He pleaded guilty to two charges:
deceiving the bank's auditors,

198
00:13:53,241 --> 00:13:55,771
and cheating the Singapore exchange.

199
00:13:56,881 --> 00:13:59,782
On the first of December
nineteen ninety-five,

200
00:13:59,802 --> 00:14:02,651
he was sentenced to six and
a half years in prison.

201
00:14:03,725 --> 00:14:05,456
He served it at Changi.

202
00:14:06,145 --> 00:14:10,576
Inside, he was diagnosed with
colon cancer and had surgery.

203
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:14,750
Britain's own inquiry
did not stop at Leeson.

204
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:18,980
Its report, published in
July nineteen ninety-five,

205
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:22,789
said the money to fund the losses
had come mainly from London,

206
00:14:23,019 --> 00:14:27,250
with no independent check on
whether the requests made sense.

207
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:33,600
Its conclusion was that Barings failed
because a number of people did not do their

208
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:34,829
jobs properly.

209
00:14:35,851 --> 00:14:40,692
The same report noted that the Singapore
auditors had judged the office's controls

210
00:14:40,711 --> 00:14:41,721
satisfactory.

211
00:14:43,072 --> 00:14:47,851
The Chancellor told Parliament that this
was not easy to reconcile with one man

212
00:14:47,871 --> 00:14:50,901
running both the trading
and the back office.

213
00:14:51,812 --> 00:14:54,421
Leeson himself was invited
to help the inquiry.

214
00:14:55,251 --> 00:14:56,051
He declined.

215
00:14:57,091 --> 00:15:01,021
The chairman of the British securities
regulator put it more simply.

216
00:15:01,891 --> 00:15:06,682
Nick Leeson pulled the trigger, he
said, but Barings gave him the gun,

217
00:15:07,091 --> 00:15:10,801
the ammunition, and then more ammunition.

218
00:15:12,073 --> 00:15:17,653
In Washington, the head of the American
futures regulator said the first three lessons

219
00:15:17,673 --> 00:15:22,103
of Barings were management
controls, management controls,

220
00:15:22,713 --> 00:15:24,343
and management controls.

221
00:15:25,539 --> 00:15:31,079
In Parliament, the opposition's Gordon Brown
asked why managers still in their jobs

222
00:15:31,099 --> 00:15:36,289
had insisted on taking their share of
ninety-five million pounds in bonuses,

223
00:15:36,699 --> 00:15:39,069
while pensioners had lost their savings.

224
00:15:40,229 --> 00:15:45,869
Leeson walked out of prison on Saturday
the third of July nineteen ninety-nine,

225
00:15:45,889 --> 00:15:50,979
his sentence cut for good behaviour, and
was put straight onto a flight to London.

226
00:15:51,908 --> 00:15:55,538
The liquidators of Barings
came with him, in a way.

227
00:15:56,229 --> 00:16:00,298
He had to give them two days' written
notice before spending money.

228
00:16:01,517 --> 00:16:06,357
His memoir, Rogue Trader, came
out in nineteen ninety-six.

229
00:16:06,377 --> 00:16:12,177
The New York Times called it a dreary book,
written by a young man very taken with

230
00:16:12,197 --> 00:16:17,107
himself, that ought to be read by banking
managers and auditors everywhere.

231
00:16:17,777 --> 00:16:20,507
It became a film starring Ewan McGregor.

232
00:16:21,037 --> 00:16:26,497
In two thousand seven, a SIMEX trading
jacket thought to be his sold for twenty-one

233
00:16:26,517 --> 00:16:28,977
thousand pounds.

234
00:16:28,997 --> 00:16:35,037
He became an after-dinner speaker on risk,
and later an investigator of financial

235
00:16:35,057 --> 00:16:35,856
misconduct.

236
00:16:36,943 --> 00:16:38,413
So here is the balance.

237
00:16:39,123 --> 00:16:41,884
One error account, opened in the
summer of nineteen ninety-two.

238
00:16:41,903 --> 00:16:47,543
Two hundred and eight million pounds
hidden by the end of nineteen ninety-four.

239
00:16:47,563 --> 00:16:53,293
Eight hundred twenty-seven
million lost by the end.

240
00:16:53,923 --> 00:16:57,364
A bank that was two hundred
and thirty-three years old,

241
00:16:57,384 --> 00:16:59,493
sold for one pound.

242
00:17:00,284 --> 00:17:02,514
The fraud was not hidden very well.

243
00:17:02,964 --> 00:17:07,495
The account had a memorable number,
the exchange had written about it,

244
00:17:07,605 --> 00:17:09,815
